HNAT learning-path regression checks

These tests are excluded from firmware and package builds. They extract the
actual C functions from the fully patched kernel/NPU sources, compile them
with bounded host kernel-API fixtures, and run with AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. A prepared kernel is required because the final
HNAT implementation includes the target's late kernel patches.

Run from the repository root:

  python3 target/linux/mediatek/tests/hnat-learning/test_learning.py \
    --kernel-tree /path/to/prepared/linux-6.12.112 \
    --npu-source package/kernel/mtk_npu/src

Coverage:

* The read-only tunnel candidate parser accepts the supported GRE and UDP
  endpoints, rejects ordinary TCP/DNS and malformed/fragmented/truncated IPv4,
  and handles linear, split and unaligned headers without changing the SKB.
* Diagnostic history skips locks when empty, expired, disabled or rejected by
  its hint. Hash collisions still require the complete CT key. Replacement
  preserves retained-record hints; the history clock handles jiffies wrap.
* Shadow storage reuses an existing record without allocation/RCU retirement,
  handles allocation failure and a concurrently retired reuse hint, rejects
  stale generations/sequences and non-UNBIND slots, and pins replaced tunnel
  CT/device references until the actual RCU retirement callback runs.
* Bulk clearing advances preparation generations for unowned slots as well
  as bound/prefilled slots. Unowned epoch changes add no byte-loss event.

The fixtures model kernel APIs; they do not execute real kernel locking, PPE
DMA, serial MIB reads or firmware. Real flow-key comparisons are fixtures in
the shadow tests, not a hardware identity proof. Kernel/NPU module builds and
board regression are separate checks.

The implementation keeps snapshot -> parse/allocate -> revalidate/publish as
three phases. Global entry_lock no longer covers ordinary header parsing or
shadow allocation. The final phase still checks the software generation,
live UNBIND key, CT ownership, output-device state and existing tunnel lease;
TX confirmation still checks generation and shadow sequence. A failed
speculative allocation/reuse hint falls back instead of bypassing accounting.

Detailed tunnel diagnostics remain enabled by default. The mtkhnat module
parameter flow_diagnostics=0 disables collection; the JSON history reports
collection_enabled. Diagnostic hints never authorize a binding or counter
owner. The necessary shared-SKB-header COW and tunnel rejection guards remain.

Accounting retains the single read-clear entry point, 100 ms interval,
default 64-slot budget and 2 ms service budget. account_poll_budget can be
selected at module load from 1 through 256; larger requested budgets remain
subject to the same service-time limit. A higher budget is not a throughput
or freshness guarantee. An in-flight MIB transaction can finish after the
service budget; it is not a hard latency bound. The accounting debugfs snapshot adds total active
owned slots, the nominal poll-only sweep duration, and last/maximum observed
sample gaps. Timestamps use monotonic milliseconds; maximum gaps are historical
since module load. Keepalive reads, scheduling and lock contention affect gaps.

The October 29 capture supplied for 24.10 establishes PPE participation for
one upload connection. Its unattributed ledger bytes are not evidence of
network packet loss and do not establish complete accounting. It is not a
25.12 runtime result. RRO firmware behavior, download acceptance, IRQ/ring/token
tuning, hardware same-tuple reuse and final in-flight counter tails still need
separate board tests; these changes do not claim a firmware reorder repair or
an exact offload fraction.
